    ($SATQ$6989c80a$SATQ$, $SATQ$bb4ec6e5-ecea-4bc4-a6d2-40a50bb5e60f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ea7edb73-7cc2-49de-8d7d-2072f97d796f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear functions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: center;"><math alttext="upper F left parenthesis x right parenthesis equals nine fifths left parenthesis x minus 273.15 right parenthesis plus 32"><mi>F</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>273.15</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>32</mn></math></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">The function <math alttext="upper F"><mi>F</mi>
</math> gives the temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, that corresponds to a temperature of <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> kelvins. If a temperature increased by <math alttext="2.10"><mn>2.10</mn>
</math> kelvins, by how much did the temperature increase, in degrees Fahrenheit?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0de59e4f-d5e6-4c4d-acd8-096ee7c3bd46","content":"<p><math alttext=\"3.78\"><mn>3.78</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"da894a94-637b-4689-b245-bffbd75df01b","content":"<p><math alttext=\"35.78\"><mn>35.78</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"8cedc234-7ffb-4361-8d1c-5d743e36fb4b","content":"<p><math alttext=\"487.89\"><mn>487.89</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"ce93aad0-1ed9-4085-b30a-24b48daa88a5","content":"<p><math alttext=\"519.89\"><mn>519.89</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0de59e4f-d5e6-4c4d-acd8-096ee7c3bd46"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the function <math alttext="upper F left parenthesis x right parenthesis equals nine fifths left parenthesis x minus 273.15 right parenthesis plus 32"><mi>F</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>273.15</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>32</mn></math> gives the temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, that corresponds to a temperature of <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> kelvins. A temperature that increased by <math alttext="2.10"><mn>2.10</mn></math> kelvins means that the value of <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> increased by <math alttext="2.10"><mn>2.10</mn></math> kelvins. It follows that an increase in <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> by <math alttext="2.10"><mn>2.10</mn></math> increases <math alttext="upper F left parenthesis x right parenthesis"><mi>F</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></math> by <math alttext="nine fifths left parenthesis 2.10 right parenthesis"><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mn>2.10</mn></mfenced></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext="3.78"><mn>3.78</mn></math>. Therefore, if a temperature increased by <math alttext="2.10"><mn>2.10</mn></math> kelvins, the temperature increased by <math alttext="3.78"><mn>3.78</mn></math> degrees Fahrenheit.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
